From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: promote the code caches in place instead of remapping

0002 keeps the three dynarec code caches as 16 KiB-aligned arrays in the module's own .bss, which
settles proximity for Xbyak's rel32 by construction. This settles permission, and it has to,
because on this console a static array that large is neither writable nor executable as loaded.

Both halves of that are already measured platform facts rather than guesses about flycast:

  - EXECUTE. The kernel refuses PROT_EXEC at map time and grants it afterwards through
    sceKernelMprotect. ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c does exactly that for mupen64plus's 32 MiB in-module
    recompiler buffer, and it does not trust the return code: sceKernelQueryMemoryProtection
    reports what a range was MAPPED with rather than what mprotect has since made it, so the
    promotion is proved by writing six bytes of x86-64 into the block and CALLING them.

  - WRITE. swanstation faulted with SEGV_ACCERR - mapped but not permitted - writing the first
    element of a static array 49 MB into its own .bss. The module loader does not hand out
    writable pages for all of a large .bss. flycast declares roughly 47 MB of it, so SH4_TCB is
    squarely in that territory. One mprotect to RWX answers both questions at once.

WHY NOT region_set_exec, WHICH IS ALREADY THERE. It rounds with PAGE_MASK - 4095 here, while the
granule is 16384 - and on failure it does not stop: control falls through to munmap() plus a fresh
mmap(), which hands back a far pointer and reproduces `offset is too big` with an extra step. A
refusal has to end the function, not redirect it.

THE THREE-BUFFER CASE NEEDED A CHANGE IN orbis_exec_mem.c ITSELF, and it is worth naming because
it would have been silent. That function was written for mupen64plus, which has one buffer, so it
cached a single answer and returned it for every later call - "asked twice means asked about the
same pages". flycast asks three times about three different ranges. Under the old rule ARM7_TCB
and the AICA DSP buffer would have been reported executable without anything ever mprotecting
them, and the first write into either would have faulted exactly the way s_fast_map did. The cache
is now per range; it is still a cache, because a repeat call for pages already promoted must not
re-run the stub check over generated code.

Left open deliberately: a refusal returns false and flycast's callers verify() on it, so a console
that will not promote these pages aborts with orbis_exec_mem's own report in the log rather than
clamping to the interpreter the way mupen64plus does. Clamping is a bigger change than this one
and is worth making only if the refusal is ever actually observed.

diff --git a/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp b/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
index 2d42410..2c43d6e 100644
--- a/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
@@ -20,6 +20,13 @@
 #define MAP_NOSYNC 0
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+/* Defined in RetroArch/ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c, which the port's build harness links into every
+ * module. Declared rather than included because that file belongs to the frontend's tree and
+ * this one is upstream's - the patch stays a patch. */
+extern "C" int orbis_exec_mem_promote(void *addr, size_t len);
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __ANDROID__
 #include <linux/ashmem.h>
 
@@ -248,6 +255,42 @@ void create_mappings(const Mapping *vmem_maps, unsigned nummaps) {
 // Prepares the code region for JIT operations, thus marking it as RWX
 bool prepare_jit_block(void *code_area, size_t size, void **code_area_rwx)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// ⚠ THE BUFFER THE CALLER BROUGHT, OR NOTHING - THERE IS NO SECOND CHOICE HERE.
+	// Every fallback below this point ends in mmap(), and on this kernel mmap answers wherever it
+	// likes: measured blocks at 0x899200000 and 0x8074d4000 against a module at 0x800870000.
+	// Xbyak emits rel32 for calls and jumps leaving generated code, so a cache more than 2 GiB
+	// from the module's text does not run slower, it throws:
+	//
+	//     libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type Xbyak::Error: offset is too big
+	//
+	// core/oslib/virtmem.h's __ORBIS__ arm therefore keeps the caches as 16 KiB-aligned arrays in
+	// the module's own .bss, where proximity is true by construction. What is left is permission.
+	//
+	// ⚠ AND region_set_exec BELOW IS NOT THE WAY TO GET IT. It mprotects a range it rounds with
+	// PAGE_MASK - 4095 here, while this console's granule is 16384 - and on failure it does not
+	// stop, it falls through to the munmap/mmap path and hands back a far pointer, which is the
+	// abort above with an extra step. It also cannot tell a granted protection from an honoured
+	// one: sceKernelQueryMemoryProtection reports what a range was MAPPED with, not what mprotect
+	// has since made it, so neither the return code nor a read-back is evidence.
+	//
+	// ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c is this port's answer and it proves the promotion by writing six bytes
+	// of x86-64 into the block and CALLING them. It also covers the other half of the platform
+	// fact: a module's own .bss this far in is not necessarily writable either - swanstation
+	// faulted with SEGV_ACCERR on a static array 49 MB into its .bss - and one mprotect to RWX
+	// answers both questions at once.
+	//
+	// Left open deliberately: a refusal returns false and flycast's callers verify() on it, so a
+	// console that will not promote these pages aborts with orbis_exec_mem's own report in the log
+	// rather than clamping to the interpreter. Clamping is a bigger change than this one and is
+	// worth making only if the refusal is ever actually observed.
+	if (code_area != nullptr && orbis_exec_mem_promote(code_area, size))
+	{
+		*code_area_rwx = code_area;
+		return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+#else
     // Try to map is as RWX, this fails apparently on OSX (and perhaps other systems?)
 	if (code_area != nullptr && region_set_exec(code_area, size))
     {
@@ -279,6 +322,7 @@ bool prepare_jit_block(void *code_area, size_t size, void **code_area_rwx)
 #endif
     *code_area_rwx = ret_ptr;
     return true;
+#endif
 }
 
 void release_jit_block(void *code_area, size_t size)
